      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "$$\n\nGiven that $t>0,$ which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t})?$",
      "worked_solution": "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t}) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t}}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t}}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{t})^{-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}+(\\sqrt{t})^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{t+1}{\\sqrt{t}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{t}}{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
      "source_grade": "Correct",
